WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.530
Some jobs are too big for one prompt, however good it is.

00:00:04.720 --> 00:00:10.470
That's where loops come in: an agent works on one goal, turn after turn, until it's done.

00:00:11.200 --> 00:00:13.610
But a loop has to know what done means.

00:00:14.280 --> 00:00:18.030
Ask for better code or a clearer report, and it goes in circles,

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:20.440
or stops early and calls it finished.

00:00:20.750 --> 00:00:23.220
It's like a revolving door with no exit:

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:26.030
lots of motion, nobody gets out.

00:00:26.300 --> 00:00:28.450
So loops fit three kinds of work.

00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:32.230
A quota one pass can't reach, like 50 test cases.

00:00:32.920 --> 00:00:35.690
Every item in a set, like a note for every account.

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:39.710
Or check, fix, recheck, until every test passes.

00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:41.630
And if it fits in one pass?

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:43.130
Skip the loop.

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:48.650
So in this adventure, you give a task a finish line a script can check, and a cap.

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:51.370
Then a fresh agent checks the work.
